This is related to what I've wanted to do with the jorne.org project. =20
I am definitely interested in being a part of the discussion. No time =20
to go to Penguicon, but I am willing to chat with the group.

Reports of Semantic Web's death have been greatly exaggerated, and I =20
can assure you that it is quite alive and well. Early adopters include =20
Best Buy, US Library of Congress, Reuters, US Census, US DoD, EU =20
governments, Google (Rich Snippets), and biomedical researchers, to =20
mention only a few who are using and publishing Linked Data and are =20
willing to put serious time and money into it. Perhaps Robin meant =20
something different by his use of "Semantic Web", but a large part of =20
Tim Berners-Lee's original vision is being implemented now in a big way.

Lojban is unique among languages in that the entire Lojban corpus =20
could become a part of the Linked Data "cloud" (see linkeddata.org). =20
To make this happen, you would:

1. parse text into predicates (moderately hard but doable)
2. convert predicates to RDF (easy enough once you have the =20
predicates, mostly this involves defining a standard URI/ID for each =20
primitive)
3. publish RDF/OWL correlations between each Lojban primitive and =20
dbpedia, wordnet, and other linked data sets (this is a manual process =20
done once for each Lojban primitive).

I posted something about this before, but I do have a very rudimentary =20
web service that takes Lojban text and returns the parse tree as XML =20
(using Robin's PEG parser). I haven't had time to work much on it, but =20
I started to look at using Jena (a Java RDF API) to produce RDF as =20
output instead. I would need help with converting the tree into =20
predicates, since my Lojban ability is limited.

See
http://groups.google.com/group/lojban/browse_thread/thread/b39f94b183cf344f
and
http://groups.google.com/group/lojban/browse_thread/thread/7e48f11c43f62b63=
/68095f3ae30b02dd

The web service is at http://jorne.org/form.jsp
Try pasting in some short text examples and let me know what you think.

